  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate (F32.1)</content>. The presenting exhaustion, poor sleep and low mood now have their diagnosis. Dr. Lindqvist-Bankole finds that he meets DSM-5-TR criteria for major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate: depressed mood, loss of interest, insomnia, fatigue, guilt and poor concentration for over two weeks, with clear impairment. Because the criteria for major depression are met, an adjustment disorder is not diagnosed. No past hypomania or mania; the Mood Disorder Questionnaire is negative. PTSD is not present: PC-PTSD-5 2, and on interview his watchfulness matches a real and continuing threat, without intrusions or avoidance. TSH and blood count were normal. PHQ-9 16. After discussing therapy alone, medicine alone, or both, and his worry about privacy, he chose both. [S]<list><item>Weekly cognitive behavioral therapy with a therapist he has chosen, including CBT for insomnia.</item><item>Sertraline 25 mg each morning for a week, then 50 mg.</item><item>Safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Insomnia disorder, comorbid with depression (G47.00)</content>. Insomnia at least four nights a week for more than three months with daytime impairment; Insomnia Severity Index 18. STOP-Bang 2 (male sex and blood pressure only; no snoring or witnessed apnea, neck 40 cm), so sleep apnea is unlikely. CBT for insomnia comes first; no hypnotic. [S]<list><item>Fixed rising time at six; bedtime no earlier than half past eleven at first; no writing in bed; no cigarettes or coffee after the evening meal.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Sustainable work plan</content>. His schedule leaves no recovery time and drives the insomnia and exhaustion. He agreed to a plan that protects his work as well as his health. [S]<list><item>One full day a week without work.</item><item>No more than two speeches a day.</item><item>Delegate travel booking and most correspondence.</item><item>A two-week break in the summer.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>At least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>16, from 17 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood Disorder Questionnaire at the initial depression workup</td><td>Once at the initial workup; repeat if hypomanic features emerge</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Negative</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PTSD screen (PC-PTSD-5) and clinical interview</td><td>At intake and if trauma symptoms emerge</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PC-PTSD-5 2 at intake; interview today finds no PTSD</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Insomnia Severity Index and sleep diary</td><td>Every visit during CBT for insomnia</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Insomnia Severity Index 18; about five hours a night by diary (score in the notes; no LOINC code used)</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Sleep apnea screen (STOP-Bang)</td><td>Once with insomnia</td><td>Completed today</td><td>STOP-Bang 2, low risk (in the notes)</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal ideation, intent or plan; PHQ-9 item 9 scored 0; expects he may be killed, without a wish to die. Safety plan reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychiatry consultation</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Lindqvist-Bankole today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>SSRI tolerability check two weeks after starting</td><td>2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Nurse telephone call in two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Office blood pressure, weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>136/86 mmHg; weight 84.2 kg; home readings planned</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Tobacco use and readiness to quit</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About a pack a day; considering a quit date</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Depression remission (PHQ-9 below 5) by six months. In progress; target 2026-09-15 [S]</item><item>Sleep of at least six and a half hours on most nights, with an Insomnia Severity Index below 10. In progress; target 2026-09-15 [S]</item><item>Smoke-free, confirmed at every visit after the quit date. In progress; target 2026-11-30 [S]</item><item>Home blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg with lifestyle changes. In progress; target 2026-12-31 [S]</item><item>A sustainable schedule: one full day a week without work, no more than two speeches a day, and a two-week break in the summer. In progress; target 2026-07-14 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Clinical Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Depression Across Three Age Cohorts (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders, hypertension and hyperlipidemia monographs (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder and Acute Stress Disorder (2023); Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder in Adults: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2016); Behavioral and psychological treatments for chronic insomnia disorder in adults: an American Academy of Sleep Medicine clinical practice guideline (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone call in two weeks. Preventive visit in three weeks. Review in six weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-12: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
